WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.148 --> 00:00:06.730
MARY KOES: Welcome
to our session

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.970
on Designing for
Observability on Google Cloud.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:11.860
Charles and I will be
your hosts for today.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.580
We are both product
managers in Cloud Logging.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.540
So before we talk about how
to design for observability,

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.930
let's take just a moment to
talk about what observability is

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.770
and why we care.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.010
The concept of
observability actually

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.010
comes from control theory, where
it's a really important concept

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:33.730
because a system can only be
controlled if it's observable,

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.040
meaning that you can
understand the internal states

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.020
from the external outputs.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.990
It's a concept we use every
day in our thermostats, car

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.180
dashboards, and more.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.220
For cloud computing,
observability

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.160
ties directly to
reliability and helps

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.270
us answer questions like,
what's this code actually doing?

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.420
Is my system healthy?

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.770
Where is my system
having problems?

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.630
What changed?

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:04.129
How can I improve my performance
and even reduce my spend?

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:08.180
So the three things you'll
need for observability

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:11.190
are logs, metrics, and traces.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.130
Since observability can
require a lot of data,

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.980
you'll also need a system
to store and organize

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.400
all this data.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.630
Fortunately, we've got that
part covered in Google Cloud

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.290
by using the same planet-scale
platform that we use internally

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:29.320
at Google to power cloud
logging and monitoring.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:30.930
I frequently get
asked by customers

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.320
if they can send a few hundred
terabytes of logs per day.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.180
We measure volumes
in quadrillions

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.300
of metrics in exabytes of logs.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.008
And now, Charles will tell
us how to get started.

00:01:43.008 --> 00:01:44.800
CHARLES BAER: Let's
talk through an example

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.420
to highlight the
observability needs

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.370
of the organization
of the product

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.650
and how it helps
you meet that needs.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.350
Imagine that you are in
charge of the DevOps or SRE

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.550
team at the company and you
just started using Google Cloud.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.010
This is a new technology for
your organization and you

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.340
professionally, though you've
worked with other cloud service

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.510
providers.

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:07.210
Your job is to develop
the observability plans

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.240
for the infrastructure
and applications.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.960
While every
organization is unique,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.100
there may be common cases
across organizations.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.300
Where specific requirements
may be a bit different,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.500
you can tailor your usage
to meet those needs.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.750
First, there's a
development use case.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.940
In our example company,
different teams

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.490
run a diverse set
of applications.

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.620
Monitoring the applications
and infrastructure

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.440
is handled by the
DevOps or SME team.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.360
Troubleshooting is
an important use case

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.030
and used to find and fix
application infrastructure

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.010
issues.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:41.480
And the last use
case is compliance.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.240
Many companies and organizations
have compliance requirements.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:47.720
Now, let's take a look at
our example, Google Cloud

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.570
infrastructure.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.030
Our cloud environment
supports different teams

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.220
in different environments--
dev, staging,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.590
implied environments--
with multiple projects

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:00.850
all centralized within a
single GCP organization.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:04.390
There are also custom-developed
application servicing end users

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.690
and there are custom and
standard business-level

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.800
applications for internal
organizational users.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.100
The environment includes
realtime analytics

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.380
and running in
Pub/Sub and BigQuery.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.170
It's a fairly
standard enterprise

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.470
for setup on Google Cloud.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.380
To design and build
observability for Google Cloud,

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.240
you need a few
[INAUDIBLE] items.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.510
First, you need to collect
the logs and metrics.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.110
Metrics and logs allow you to
build dashboards and alerting

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.200
for your monitoring and
troubleshooting use cases.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.007
If you can instrument
your applications,

00:03:37.007 --> 00:03:38.590
you can add support
for tracing, which

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.780
allows you to see
the performance

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.030
and optimize your
application across services.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.548
Once you collect the logs
and the metrics and traces,

00:03:48.548 --> 00:03:50.590
you then need a tool that
will help you interpret

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.690
those signals, which
addresses all the use

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.820
cases-- the developer,
monitoring, troubleshooting,

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.180
and compliance.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.570
This is exactly what the
Google Cloud operation

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.760
suite provides to you.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.890
And now, there's an
exciting new feature

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:05.200
in Cloud Logging in the
cloud operation suite

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.580
that we'd like to
share with you.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.200
MARY KOES: Thanks, Charles.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.840
So one of our key
goals for observability

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:15.720
is being able to ask
questions and find answers.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.720
And to do that, you often need
the data to be stored together.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.740
It's one of the reasons that
our architecture uniting

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.590
cloud monitoring, logging, and
trace into the cloud operations

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.660
suite is so powerful.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.780
Well, since we launched the
Cloud Logging router last fall

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.910
to give transparency
and control over

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.400
the logs you want to
keep for observability,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.200
and also to build an open
ecosystem for our partners,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.490
we've gotten great feedback
from our users and partners.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:44.430
We've heard that you love being
able to use log syncs to send

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:47.790
data to different destinations,
and exclusions to fine

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.480
tune that volume of data.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.560
But we've also heard that
you'd like that same ability

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.280
to send logs to Cloud Logging
in a different project

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.760
so that you can centralize
your logs within Cloud Logging.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.040
We're really excited
to announce today

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.620
a number of new log
management features

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.110
that can help improve
your observability,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.570
starting with
expanded functionality

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.250
for the logs router.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.210
You can now use the full
power of the logs router,

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:17.640
send logs from Projects
A and B to Project C,

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.700
including setting up a sync
at the folder or org level

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.830
so that all the child
projects are inherited.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.210
This is really great for
security applications.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.000
We do this by
introducing log buckets

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.030
as a first-class
storage solution.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.270
Using log buckets, you can
centralize or subdivide

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.610
your logs based on your needs.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.530
From the name, log
buckets may look

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.330
like cloud storage buckets.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.490
But they're built on
the same logging tech

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.280
stack that we've been using to
deliver your logs in real time,

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:53.420
including advanced indexing and
optimizations for timestamps.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.180
Log buckets also allow
you to take advantage

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.240
of advanced management
capabilities, including

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.020
customer retention, which we've
just made generally available,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.610
and also newly launched
regionalization to log buckets.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.490
One of my favorite things about
log buckets and the expanded

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:15.030
logs router functionality is
that we preserve that great

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.380
in-context experience, and we do
magic behind the scenes to look

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:23.340
up where your logs are stored so
you can still view those logs--

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.890
for your Kubernetes
cluster, for example--

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.440
right in the GKE
console in Project A

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:32.380
even if they're stored
centrally in Project C.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.840
Finally, for more
advanced users,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:38.110
we're also introducing
log views to allow

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:41.560
you to subdivide access to
logs within a log bucket.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.050
So, for example, Charles
may centralize the logs

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.090
from all of these
projects but only

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.280
grant me access to
logs from Project A

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.080
while he can search
across all logs.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.876
Charles, over to you to show how
to pull all of this together.

00:06:54.876 --> 00:06:56.350
CHARLES BAER: Thank you, Mary.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:58.400
Our design approach
is an iterative one,

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.700
starting with what's
provided and progressing

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.490
through the in-depth
configuration.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:06.010
A great place to start is
the out-of-the-box logs

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:06.910
and metrics.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.018
Cloud Logging collects all of
your logs in cloud services,

00:07:10.018 --> 00:07:11.560
and even auto logs
so you know what's

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.970
going on in the platform.

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:16.720
For Compute Engine, if you need
additional application logs,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.320
you can install agents
to collect those logs.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.790
Cloud monitoring,
similarly, collects metrics

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.840
from all the cloud
services by default

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.020
so you can build
dashboards and learning.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.700
When you need
additional application

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:31.120
or business-level metrics, you
can use those custom metrics

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.270
to monitor those over time.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:36.700
Once you're using the
out-of-the-box metrics

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:39.550
and logs, you may find that you
need specific application logs

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:40.900
or business metrics.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:45.430
You can collect those by using
logging and monitoring agents.

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:48.180
Next, you can customize
your experience,

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.940
and you can do that by using
logs-based metrics, which

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:55.150
can translate your log
messages into metrics.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.570
Then you can use those metrics
to create specific learning

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:01.030
based on your
observability requirements

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.960
for your environment.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.590
Now, with all of that
done, the next challenge

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.470
is doing so at scale.

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.540
Cloud Logging and
monitoring both provide

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.290
ways to aggregate
your logs and metrics.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:15.970
With monitoring workspaces,
you can group your projects

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.550
into a single place and
monitor all of your projects

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.650
in a centralized location.

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:23.560
With logs router, log
buckets, and log views,

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.260
that Mary mentioned, you
can direct all of your logs

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.920
to a centralized project
or set of projects.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.810
Now, if we go back
to our example,

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:34.480
I'll demo the step by step,
the set of best practices

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.169
in cloud logging and monitoring.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:40.850
First, I'll show you what
logging and monitoring provides

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:41.475
out of the box.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:47.780
To build observability,
you need to start

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.210
with a collection of logs
and metrics that you need.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.010
First, I'll show you
the logs and metrics

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.610
that cloud monitoring and Cloud
Logging collect out of the box.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:59.630
Let's start with
Cloud Monitoring.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.270
Cloud Monitoring automatically
collects infrastructure

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.130
and service-level metrics.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.440
Out-of-the-box Cloud Monitoring
drives the summary view

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:07.880
of your environment.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:11.090
Here, you can see the incidents,
the charts, dashboards,

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:12.660
and your uptime check.

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.600
First, let's take a look
at one of the default

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.590
dashboards, the VM instances.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.910
This newly refreshed dashboard
provides a high level view

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.680
of your VM instances.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.150
Here, you can see
a detailed view

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.580
of all the instances
in your environments

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.190
along with useful
details about each.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.200
In this particular
GCP environment,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.180
you'll see several app
servers, a GKE cluster,

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.560
a Jenkins VM for CICD,
two Minecraft servers,

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.650
and a web development server.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:41.400
Now, we're running
these Minecraft servers

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.270
because we happen to like
Minecraft, and it's fun.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.708
And also, it's a great
example of running a Java app

00:09:46.708 --> 00:09:48.750
on instance, because
Minecraft-- the version that

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:49.500
we're running--

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.570
is actually running on Java.

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.030
So we think this
is a fun example.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:54.530
And it's the same
type of experience

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:58.140
you have running any enterprise
Java app analytic server.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:00.580
Now, the Overview tab provides
a quick reference and set

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:01.300
of charts.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:04.210
And each of the tabs
provides useful information

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:06.650
about your fleet of VMs.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.840
Going back to the
Inventory tab, you'll

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:11.720
notice that some of the VMs have
additional memory utilization

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.003
information.

00:10:13.003 --> 00:10:14.420
This is provided
by the monitoring

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:16.527
agent, which collects
additional information.

00:10:16.527 --> 00:10:19.110
To get a sense of the additional
information that's collected,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:20.818
first, let's look at
the Minecraft Server

00:10:20.818 --> 00:10:25.420
2, which doesn't have the
monitoring agents installed.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.110
Notice that there's
information about the instance,

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:29.850
and there's a default
set of charts that

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:32.590
are available for the instance.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.400
Now, let's go look and
see what information

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.820
is provided for
the first instance,

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:42.310
clicking on Minecraft Server 1.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:46.140
Notice that there are now a set
of processes that are listed

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:47.250
for the instance as well.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.708
So these are all
the processes that

00:10:48.708 --> 00:10:50.580
are running on the instance.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.220
And the second thing to notice
is that we have an agent

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:54.220
up here.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.640
And the agent provides
additional set

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.870
of charts and dashboards
around memory utilization,

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:03.612
disk utilization, and
detailed CPU information.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:09.640
What's useful is that you
can start without the agent.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.220
And then whenever you need
this process-level information

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:14.080
or the granular
memory information,

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.640
you can install the agent.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.130
I'll show you how to do
this later in the demo.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:20.230
Next, I'll show you the
out-of-the-box logging

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:21.132
experience.

00:11:24.289 --> 00:11:26.000
Cloud logging
automatically collects

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.540
your platform logs and your
admin activity audit logs.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.340
The Logs Viewer
gives you the ability

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.650
to search across your logs
and provides a query builder

00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:36.237
to help you build your queries.

00:11:36.237 --> 00:11:37.820
This is the new Logs
Viewer experience

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.403
that we're using
today, which includes

00:11:39.403 --> 00:11:41.080
useful updates,
including the Logs Field

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.512
Explorer and the histogram.

00:11:43.512 --> 00:11:45.640
The Logs Field Explorer
helps you easily

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.840
refine your search by
picking out top level fields

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.550
and values that you can
easily add to your queries.

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:54.780
When you select a GCE
instance resource type,

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:56.705
the query runs, and
the Logs Field Explorer

00:11:56.705 --> 00:12:00.120
is updated with a
subset of the results.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.010
The histogram helps
you gain insights

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:05.410
into the frequency of the
log entries over time,

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:09.390
and jump to the specific
logs for a given time window.

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.890
Both the Logs Field Explorer
and the histogram may be closed

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:14.190
and added back via
the page layout.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:17.260
OK, so let's take a
look at some logs.

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.660
First, we'll look
at an audit log.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:21.340
You can easily
refine your search

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:23.650
to find the audit logs
using Log Field Explorer

00:12:23.650 --> 00:12:29.610
and selecting the audit
logs that are listed here.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.450
Cloud Logging collects audit
logs for your services, which

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.550
include admin activities.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.550
This captures the who
did what, where, and when

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.550
for your Google Cloud services.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:41.380
OK.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:44.890
So now that you have the
logs through admin activities

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:48.370
for the VM instance
within the project, first,

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:52.300
we can look for the first
operation in the audit log.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:55.060
And you can match the entries
for the particular request

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.090
by looking at the
particular operation

00:12:58.090 --> 00:13:01.940
and then clicking to
show matching entries.

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.360
Now, we have all the audit logs
for this particular request.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.340
Next, we can see
who made the request

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.320
by looking at the proto
payload and looking

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:10.860
at the authentication fields.

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:15.220
So we'll expand the log and
look at the authentication

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:16.256
information.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:23.120
Next, you can see
who made the request

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.930
by looking at the proto
payload and the request type.

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:30.800
Here, we can see that it
is compute instances stop.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:34.370
So in addition to audit logs,
logging also captures your logs

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.172
from GCP services.

00:13:36.172 --> 00:13:37.880
What isn't collected,
though, by default,

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:41.630
are logs written to specific
log files or the syslog.

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.180
To provide this
flexibility, though, you

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.700
can install the logging
agents to collect your syslogs

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.770
and adjust your
application logs.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.780
You can see an example
of these application logs

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:55.340
by clearing your query filter
and leaving the GC instance,

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:58.000
and then selecting, for
example, the Minecraft logs.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:07.010
We select a log entry
and then expand it,

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.500
and see all the details
for the specific log entry,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.650
including the metadata, as
well as the data generated

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.230
from the application itself.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:19.170
These logs are
application-specific

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:21.060
and created by the
Java app, which can

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:24.610
be collected using the agent.

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:26.800
Next, I'll show you how
you can install the agents

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:28.880
and collect the additional
log and metrics.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.350
Now let's take a look.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:34.090
In our example, we saw
that Minecraft Server 1 had

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:36.480
the monitoring and
logging agents installed,

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:38.880
and Minecraft Server 2 didn't.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.620
The agents are built by Google
on top of the open source

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:44.050
Fluentd and then
collect D projects.

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:46.110
So let's install
the agents and start

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:49.320
collecting Minecraft logs.

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:52.110
First, we'll start with
the monitoring agent.

00:14:56.062 --> 00:14:59.390
This will just take a
minute, and then we'll

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:00.940
move on to the logging agent.

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:05.730
Great.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:08.315
Now, we'll install the
logging agents as well.

00:15:08.315 --> 00:15:09.690
And you can find
the instructions

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:15.380
to install these agents
directly on our documentation.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:17.240
Once they're done,
we'll check to make sure

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:20.690
that the logging agents have
been successfully installed

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:22.820
and start it using
the service command.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:25.850
So there is the
Stackdriver agent,

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:27.830
which is the monitoring agent.

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:29.960
And then we'll look
at the Fluentd agent,

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:31.550
which is the logging agents.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:34.430
Both of them are
running successfully.

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.860
Now, for fun, we'll
also customize the logs

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:39.800
that the Fluentd agent collects
to specifically collect

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:42.290
the Minecraft log
from the instance.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:45.410
And so to do that, we will
use a specific configuration

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:49.560
file that I have
created, and we will then

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:52.730
copy that to the directory
that contains the Fluentd

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:53.550
configuration.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:16:00.577
And then we will
restart the agent

00:16:00.577 --> 00:16:02.035
and we'll wait for
that to restart.

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.940
Now that it's restarted,
we'll take a look

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.945
at the configuration
file that I use.

00:16:11.945 --> 00:16:13.320
The first thing
to notice here is

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.890
the path to the log, which
is the Minecraft log here.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.515
And the next thing
to notice is the tag

00:16:19.515 --> 00:16:21.348
that we give it, which
is the Minecraft log.

00:16:21.348 --> 00:16:25.065
And this is the entry that
will appear in Cloud Logging.

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:35.270
In this example, we're
doing something fun,

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:37.170
like running a Minecraft server.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:38.840
However, these are
the same requirements

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:41.120
that you have when you're
running your own application

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.030
or instance with your
own enterprise software.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.230
To show how logging is
collecting the logs,

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.920
I'll show a command to the
Minecraft instance that

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.570
will appear in the
logs, and then I

00:16:50.570 --> 00:16:54.500
can find that command in
the newly tailed logs.

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:59.920
So enter the Minecraft server,
and I will set the time to 0.

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:03.665
And once I do that, I
can exit the server here.

00:17:03.665 --> 00:17:05.290
Now that we have the
process and memory

00:17:05.290 --> 00:17:07.810
metrics from the monitoring
agent and assist logs

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:09.901
and the Minecraft
logs for the agent,

00:17:09.901 --> 00:17:11.609
now I'll show you the
logs in the viewer.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.250
This time, I'll use
the query builder

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:29.930
to query for my virtual machine.

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:34.780
So here, I'll scroll down
to find the VM instance

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.950
and I'll look for the
Minecraft 2 server here.

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:39.710
There it is.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:43.660
And then I'll add
that to my query.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:46.340
I'll run my query, and then
I'll use the Logs Field

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:51.950
Explorer to hone in on the
specific Minecraft logs here.

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:53.570
So there we see,
if we scroll down,

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:55.930
we actually see the time
that we've set to 0.

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.900
So if I expand this log,
I can show you the fields.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:17:59.910
Very similar.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.710
You can see that it's coming
from the Minecraft 2 server,

00:18:02.710 --> 00:18:07.040
and it contains the same
text that I had just entered.

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:10.150
So now you can see that we have
Minecraft logs, the Minecraft 2

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:12.130
server, and the message
that we've entered.

00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:15.430
And we've collected that
using the logging agent.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:18.070
Next, I'll show you how you
can customize your environment

00:18:18.070 --> 00:18:20.410
by creating log-based
metrics and alerting.

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.040
Now, that we've collected
all of our logs,

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:28.320
let's start making them
actionable with custom metrics

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:29.700
and alerting.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.070
I'll show you how to create
a logs-based metric based

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:35.080
on the count of the messages
from the application.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.140
For the example, I'll
count the number of times

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:39.240
that the server saves the game.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.000
This should be a regular
process and log entry.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:44.210
If there are no logs from
the process over time,

00:18:44.210 --> 00:18:47.800
then that should trigger a
check on the application server.

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:50.067
Starting Logs Viewer, let's
add the specific query

00:18:50.067 --> 00:18:51.900
to return the logs that
we're interested in.

00:18:56.800 --> 00:19:00.495
Next, we'll click on the Action
and then click Create Metric.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:07.970
This will load the
logs-based metric UI.

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:14.420
We can give the metric
a name, and then we

00:19:14.420 --> 00:19:15.350
can create the metric.

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:21.830
Now once we've done that, we
can actually click on the metric

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:25.820
that we just created here,
and we can view the logs.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.910
We can view the metric
in the metric explorer

00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:30.330
to see the details.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:32.520
And we even create an
alert from that metric.

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:36.020
So first, why don't we
look at another metric

00:19:36.020 --> 00:19:38.090
that I've created
previously, since we just

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:40.700
started correcting this one,
and we'll look at the Metrics

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:44.120
Explorer for this entry.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.790
So this is the Metric Explorer.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:52.220
This is the interface you
use in Cloud Monitoring

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:56.735
if you would like to do ad hoc
analysis over your metrics.

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:09.080
So now we can see the regular
occurrence of the metric

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:11.900
over time.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:15.447
This metric is useful for
dashboards and ad hoc analysis.

00:20:15.447 --> 00:20:17.030
On top of that, we
can create an alert

00:20:17.030 --> 00:20:20.270
to alert us whenever the
specific condition happened.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:21.740
I'll show you how
to do that now.

00:20:27.053 --> 00:20:31.280
We can go back to
the login console,

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:33.230
and we can look at
the logs-based metrics

00:20:33.230 --> 00:20:36.010
that we have that we
created just a moment ago.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.690
And then we can create an
alert for the specific metric.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.220
In this case, I
will use the metric

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:43.220
that I created previously--

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:44.430
create an alert for metric.

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:50.220
And this will allow me to
specify the conditions here.

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:52.800
So I will specify
the right aggregator.

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.490
So this helps me identify
the values of the metric

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:57.960
that I'd like to use.

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:03.660
And we can say is below two
over a given threshold of time.

00:21:03.660 --> 00:21:08.755
In this case, we'll use six
hours as our time frame.

00:21:08.755 --> 00:21:11.530
We will save the alert here.

00:21:11.530 --> 00:21:13.720
This will ask us
to name the alert.

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.470
And then we have the option of
adding a notification channel,

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.500
such as an email or
any of the options

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:27.150
here, whether those be
Slack or SMS or a webhook,

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:28.990
or even Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:21:28.990 --> 00:21:31.150
I won't add a
notification channel here.

00:21:31.150 --> 00:21:33.220
We also have the ability
to add documentation.

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:36.330
Once you do that,
you've saved your alert.

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:38.680
So while we're having fun
with the Minecraft example,

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:41.100
the same process applies
to monitoring the errors

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:44.190
from your application
or the ProQuest volume

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:49.100
or the CPU utilization
for your instances.

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:51.920
Next, I'll show you how you
can centralize your logging

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:55.470
and monitoring.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:57.450
Now that we have
collected all of our logs

00:21:57.450 --> 00:22:00.000
and we've built alerting to
customize our environment,

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.270
the next step is to centralize
both logging and monitoring.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.980
You'll likely have
more than one project,

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:06.563
and centralized
logging and monitoring

00:22:06.563 --> 00:22:08.700
makes it easier to find
them, makes it easier

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:10.440
to satisfy operations
use case, and even

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:12.750
meet your compliance
requirements.

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:15.390
Log centralization is provided
by the new feature that Mary

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:17.920
mentioned, logging buckets.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:21.370
The logging storage sections
contains the logging buckets,

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:24.780
which is a logical container for
your logs inside your logging

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:25.922
project.

00:22:25.922 --> 00:22:28.380
In this case, I'd like to send
all of my logs for the three

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.200
projects in my organization
to this centralized logging

00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:32.520
project.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:34.820
To do that, I've
created a single bucket

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:39.260
called All Production Logs
to hold my logs for all three

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:41.280
projects.

00:22:41.280 --> 00:22:44.540
And the way that the logs
are delivered to this project

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:47.000
is through a logging sync.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.070
So now, I will show
you the logging sync

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:54.810
in the other project, which
delivers the logs to this one.

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:58.790
Here, this is the original
project I was showing you.

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:02.510
And now we have a logging
sync, which essentially takes

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:07.670
all of the logs that is
specified based on your logging

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:12.350
sync, and sends them to the
destination that you select.

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:15.110
In this case, we
are sending them

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:17.860
to the logs in the
central logging project

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:19.445
under All Production Logs.

00:23:19.445 --> 00:23:21.980
And if we look at
the sync itself,

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:25.840
we can see that I have it
sending it to Cloud Logging.

00:23:25.840 --> 00:23:27.650
Here is the
particular destination

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.630
in the other project.

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.730
We have not added any filter,
so all logs will be sent.

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:38.010
And then we have not opted any
logs out of the filter as well.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.660
So this will generate logs
in our centralized logging

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:43.520
project.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.310
So let's go take a look at the
logs in our centralized logging

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:46.810
project.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:57.380
So once I have the logs
in a centralized place,

00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.450
I can issue queries
across all the logs.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:02.240
But first, I have to refine
the scope of the query

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:04.040
and select the appropriate
logging bucket.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:06.830
I can do that by using
the refined scope

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:09.860
and then selecting the
scope by storage, and then

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:14.770
a centralized logging bucket,
and then clicking Apply.

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:18.580
For example, if I select
the Kubernetes details,

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:21.640
I can see all of the
Kubernetes resources

00:24:21.640 --> 00:24:24.820
within the MS demo app,
including the namespaces

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:25.720
and the pod names.

00:24:30.130 --> 00:24:32.800
When I select the
VM instances, I

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:37.420
can see all of the logs that
are generated from the instances

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:39.715
across all three
of the projects.

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:44.088
Now for the
centralized monitoring,

00:24:44.088 --> 00:24:45.880
I'll go back to the
Monitoring Summary page

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:46.963
and show you the settings.

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:57.160
In monitoring, there's a
concept of a workspace, which

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:59.290
can monitor multiple projects.

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:04.520
I've added each of these three
projects into the workspace.

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:07.240
Now by doing so, I can
aggregate monitoring

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.610
across any of the
metrics in the projects.

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:11.860
I can create dashboards,
alerting, uptime checks

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:14.650
for any of the metrics captured
in any of the projects.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:15.737
And that's it.

00:25:15.737 --> 00:25:18.070
Now, we've got all of our
logs and all of our monitoring

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:20.045
metrics in the same place.

00:25:20.045 --> 00:25:21.670
With this configuration,
you can search

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:22.810
across your project logs.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:24.670
You can aggregate metrics
in your dashboards

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:27.160
for all of your projects.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.720
I started out by showing you
the out-of-the-box metrics

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:33.280
and the logs collected by Cloud
Monitoring and Cloud Logging.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:37.130
Next, I added agents to
collect the additional metrics

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.360
and logs.

00:25:38.360 --> 00:25:41.030
Then I customized for my own
observability requirements

00:25:41.030 --> 00:25:44.390
by building a log-based
metric and associated load.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:47.060
Lastly, I configured all three
projects in my organization

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:50.060
to collect logs
into a single bucket

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:53.450
and send that to a
single workspace.

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:55.580
We built a baseline
for observability,

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:57.590
which can be extended
by instrumenting

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:00.830
your code for distributed
tracing and performance

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:03.588
optimization.

00:26:03.588 --> 00:26:05.130
To learn more about
how the Cloud Ops

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:08.600
suite can help you develop
observability for your systems,

